From 0f5c7857731822576f8a144c424c62c26f41e54a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Boris Brezillon <boris.brezillon@collabora.com>
Date: Thu, 23 Apr 2026 09:44:09 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 5/6] panfrost: Move BO access tracking to the context layer

With multi-context being able to concurrently access pipe_resource
objects, it becomes unsafe to have a panfrost_bo::gpu_access flag
that's encoding any access across all contexts. One way would be to
protect this ::gpu_access field and the wait logic that's using it
with a lock, but then it would serialize waits across contexts, even
though they might have different types (writers-only vs all) and
different timeouts.

The other option is to track accesses per-context, and turn
panfrost_bo_wait() into a pure KMD non-optimized wait, which is what
this commit is doing.

There's a few things to note here:

- the panfrost_bo_wait() that's done in the fetch_from_cache path is no
  longer optimized to avoid the IOCTL when the BO has no pending access.
  It's probably okay because we're doing a bunch of other IOCTLs in that
  path already, so if it was syscall bound, it would already be a problem
- we were previously flagging internal BOs with pending RW accesses in
  csf_attach_sync_points() and panfrost_pool_get_bo_handles(). With
  pan_bo now unconditionally calling pan_kmod_bo_wait(), this is no
  longer needed, because we know these per-batch mem pools will get
  recycled through the pan_bo cache.

